 "the_prediction_and_why_it_was_wrong": "docs/T3_SUBMISSION.md registered, before launch, that pass 1 would land in 0.10-0.20 and BELOW the raw reconstruction's 0.228, reasoning from the Tier 1 result where a 1 cm reference swap cost 0.465 -> 0.289. It came in at 0.23233, slightly ABOVE raw, with MPPE better (62.11 vs 66.60). The reasoning missed that a physical policy cannot reproduce a physically impossible input: where the reconstruction has the object floating or jittering, contact and gravity correct it. That is why AUC and MPPE improve.",
 "but_the_success_rates_went_the_other_way": "SP-SR 0.333 -> 0.000 and MP-SR 0.033 -> 0.000. Those thresholds are tight (10 cm / 0.5 rad and 3 cm / 30 deg, time-averaged) and the policy's own tracking error pushes the well-reconstructed clips past them. So the raw reconstruction would beat this entry on two of the four Tier 3 boards, and cannot be submitted on any of them.",
 "what_this_means_for_spending": "Tier 3's bottleneck is the reconstruction, not the policy. The policy already scores at the reconstruction's own level (0.2323 vs 0.2280), so GPU spent on better Tier 3 policies buys little; the lever is the 20 of 30 clips whose reconstruction loses the object, and that is free CPU work. No GPU is allocated to Tier 3.",
